<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/laptop-review.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/laptop-review-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/laptop-review.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/claim-dispute-services/">Services</a><span>/</span>Claim Estimate Review</nav><p class="kicker lt">Claim dispute service</p><h1>Insurance Claim Estimate Review in SC</h1><p class="lede">Before you accept a payment or demand appraisal, it helps to know exactly where the insurer’s estimate falls short. We review it line by line and tell you what we find, including when the estimate looks fair.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn cu" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ol" href="/contact-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts"><div><small>Best for</small><b>Deciding next steps</b></div><div><small>Output</small><b>Written gap summary</b></div><div><small>Useful before</small><b>Signing or appraisal</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>What an insurance claim estimate review in SC includes</h2>
<p>An insurance claim estimate review in SC is a detailed check of the estimate your insurer wrote, compared against the actual damage, local pricing and the way a contractor would do the work. It gives you a clear picture of the gap before you decide whether to accept the payment, ask for a supplement, or demand appraisal.</p>
<p>Many owners only see a summary page with a total and a depreciation figure. The full estimate, often dozens of pages, tells you much more: what was measured, what was left out and how each item was priced.</p>
<h2>How we review an estimate</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Scope.</strong> We compare the estimate against photos, measurements and, where needed, our own inspection, to find missing rooms, slopes, elevations or trades.</li>
<li><strong>Quantities.</strong> We check roof squares, wall and floor areas, and linear feet of trim, gutters and siding.</li>
<li><strong>Pricing.</strong> We check unit costs against current local pricing for your area, whether that is Rock Hill, Florence or Hilton Head Island.</li>
<li><strong>Code and access.</strong> We look for missing code items and steep, high or access charges.</li>
<li><strong>Overhead, profit and depreciation.</strong> We check whether these are applied consistently with the policy and the job.</li>
<li><strong>Written summary.</strong> You get a plain-language list of the main gaps and an estimate of their value.</li>
</ol>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Honest answers</b>Sometimes the insurer’s estimate is close to fair. When it is, we will tell you, so you don’t spend money on an appraisal that won’t change much.</div>
<h2>Common gaps we find in estimates</h2>
<ul>
<li>Roof slopes or elevations left out entirely.</li>
<li>No drip edge, starter strip or ice and water barrier where code or manufacturer specifications require them.</li>
<li>Paint priced for one wall instead of the whole room.</li>
<li>Flooring that stops at a doorway when the floor runs continuously.</li>
<li>Depreciation applied to labor in ways that may not match the policy.</li>
<li>Missing permits, dumpsters, protection of contents or cleanup.</li>
<li>Pricing that lags behind the post-storm market after events like Hurricane Helene in the Upstate or Ian on the Grand Strand.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Documents we need for a review</h2>
<p>Send us the full insurer estimate, not just the summary; the policy declarations and any endorsements; your photos; and any contractor bids. If you’ve already had partial payments, send those letters too. A quick pass through the <a href="/free-claim-tools/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap and fee estimator</a> can help you decide whether a full review is worthwhile.</p>
<h2>Who benefits most from an estimate review</h2>
<p>Reviews are most useful for owners who received a payment that seems low but aren’t sure why, owners weighing a contractor’s bid against the insurer’s number, and associations or businesses that need a written summary for a board, lender or partner. They are also useful before a deadline, such as a date by which repairs must be completed to recover depreciation.</p>
<p>A review is also a good way to learn the language of estimates. Once you understand line items, unit prices and depreciation, conversations with your adjuster become far more productive, whether or not you ever go to appraisal. Our <a href="/appraisal-glossary/">appraisal glossary</a> explains common terms you will see in the estimate.</p>
<h2>When a review is not enough, or not needed</h2>
<p>A review shows you where the gaps are, but it doesn’t force the insurer to pay. If the carrier won’t move on a well-supported supplement, appraisal may be the next step; see <a href="/claim-dispute-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">appraisal demand representation</a>. If the problem is a denial of coverage rather than a pricing gap, a review won’t resolve it, and a South Carolina attorney is the better resource. Complaints about how a claim is being handled can be directed to the SCDOI.</p>

<h2>Questions about claim estimate review</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do I need a review before demanding appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>It is not required, but it helps. A review shows the size and source of the gap, so you can decide whether appraisal is worth the cost.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can you review a contractor’s estimate as well as the insurer’s?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. Comparing both estimates side by side often shows exactly where the disagreement lies.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will a review guarantee a higher payment?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. A review identifies gaps and supports a request for more, but the insurer or an appraisal panel decides what is paid.</p></div></details></div>
